      "So the real fork is play-in-2026 versus wait-for-PC. If you want Vice City on 19 November 2026, you need a PS5 or Xbox Series machine and a console edition. That path is documented in /guides/gta-6-preorder-guide (platform, physical versus digital, Collector discipline) and /guides/best-setup-gta-6-ps5-xbox (storage, headset, display). If you are willing to skip the console year, keep your PC money liquid and ignore launch-week GPU panic that cites unpublished requirements.",
      "Console pre-orders do not become PC keys. Amazon, Fnac, PlayStation Store, and Microsoft Store carts that say PS5 or Xbox are console carts. Cancel-until-ship norms on many retailer pre-orders are useful if you change platforms — they are not a bridge to Windows. If a seller implies otherwise, walk away. Map-6 product cards, when they light up, are tagged by intent; they will not pretend a DualSense listing is a Steam depot.",
      "PC hardware FOMO is the second trap. No official recommended specs exist yet — see the companion unofficial-ranges guide if you want planning brackets clearly labeled as unofficial. Buying a card because a YouTuber invented “RTX required for Leonida rain” is how people own RGB and still cannot play in November. If you might buy a console for launch, spend on the living-room bottlenecks first: free SSD space, a spare pad, a headset, HDMI 2.1 readiness. That checklist lives on the setup guide, not on a fake PC Q&A.",
      "While the PC date is empty, geography is not. Open /map (or /en/map), filter Landmarks while you scrub trailers, switch Collectibles only when you are planning routes rather than hunting rumor pins, and copy HUD X/Y into notes. Regional hubs under /locations — Vice City, Ocean Drive, Leonida Keys, Port Gellhorn, Grassrivers, Ambrosia Island, Mount Kalaga — teach relative position. Cartography lineage includes GTADB community assets under CC BY 4.0 where noted. Waiting for Steam is a fine plan; refreshing empty PC product pages is not.",
