      "Storage is the spec you can act on without a PC date. Console GTA 6 will live on machines built around fast internal SSDs; a future PC build that installs the same world on a dying SATA platter is a bad bet. Rockstar has not published a PC download size. GTA V’s current footprint is already past the old “50 GB is huge” era — plan a large NVMe and leave headroom for patches and captures. Filling a 512 GB boot drive to the last gigabyte is how installs fail. If you play on console in 2026 while you wait for PC, the same storage lesson applies: see /guides/best-setup-gta-6-ps5-xbox for console-compatible SSD and expansion-card thinking.",
      "Displays and audio are living-room decisions, not PC-Q waiting rooms. A 120 Hz HDMI 2.1 panel helps console performance modes if and when Rockstar ships them on 19 November 2026; it will still be a good monitor when a PC version arrives. You do not need OLED to enjoy Vice City. You do need the cable in the correct HDMI port and Game Mode on. A wireless headset that works on the console you actually own matters more than a phantom “PC 7.1 for Lucia’s footsteps” SKU. Those intents — storage, display, headset — are the honest hardware funnel on this page while PC requirements are unpublished.",
      "What this page will not do: recommend a console upgrade as if it were a PC part, invent VRAM floors as official, or list collector-edition GPU bundles Rockstar has not sold. If you need the “should I buy a PS5 or Series X to play in 2026” ticket, that is /guides/gta-6-ps5-vs-xbox plus /guides/best-setup-gta-6-ps5-xbox — not a hidden console_upgrade pitch disguised as a spec sheet. PC wait and console-now can coexist: keep the desktop for 2027-or-whenever, kit the TV for November.",
